const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Manuscript Editing","serviceType":"Editing service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/manuscript-editing/","description":"Copyediting plus formatting to one named journal — its own deviations, the word limit, the figures and the submission checklist. From $500.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What does manuscript editing include, and where does it stop?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Two things: a copyedit, and formatting to one named journal.\n\nThe copyedit is “correcting spelling, grammar, usage, and punctuation, checking cross-references, and preparing the style sheets that guide consistency and accuracy” — at $0.035 a word. The formatting is your target journal’s instructions read in full, including every deviation the journal makes from whichever named style it claims to use. On our sample, twenty-three of them.\n\nWhere it stops is the argument. Restructuring a paper, rewriting a discussion, strengthening a claim — that is substantive or developmental editing at $0.075 or $0.100 a word on our scientific editing page, and we tell you which you need before quoting rather than after delivering."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will editing improve my chances of journal acceptance?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not directly. Acceptance is an editor’s judgement about your research — its novelty, its design, its fit with the journal — and no editing service touches any of those.\n\nWhat formatting does is narrower: it removes the things that stop a paper before anyone engages with it. A manuscript over the word limit, a four-heading abstract where the journal wants five, references in a style it does not use, or a reporting checklist it requires and did not receive — each is a reason to return a paper that has nothing to do with the science in it.\n\nThat is worthwhile. It is not the same as improving a paper’s chances."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"My paper is over the word limit. Will you cut it?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Up to a point, and then we stop and hand you a measurement.\n\nCompression reliably recovers 10 to 20% without losing anything, from three places in this order. A discussion that restates the results before discussing them — on our sample, 740 words. A methods section describing standard procedures at a length the journal’s own papers do not use — 410 words. And stacked hedging — 280 words across 61 instances, reduced to one each rather than removed, because the caution belongs to the authors.\n\nThat recovered 1,430 words and left the paper 470 over. At that point we sent the length audit and did not cut: which material leaves a paper is a decision about what the paper argues."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Which reference styles do you work in, and are they current?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Vancouver, AMA, APA, Chicago, CSE, ACS, IEEE, MLA and ASA — each at its current edition, which matters because four moved recently and templates do not.\n\nChicago is on its 18th edition, September 2024. CSE’s ninth, May 2024, was retitled — it is now The CSE Manual. The ACS Style Guide has been superseded by the continuously updated ACS Guide to Scholarly Communication. AMA is on the 11th, March 2020, with rolling policy updates the printed book does not contain.\n\nVancouver means the ICMJE Recommendations, updated January 2026, plus the NLM’s Citing Medicine. The name “Uniform Requirements for Manuscripts” was retired in August 2013."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How should I supply my figures?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As source files, not as images pasted into the manuscript — the spreadsheet, the plotting script, the vector file. A chart exported at screen resolution cannot be raised to a journal’s 300 or 600 dpi afterwards, because the data is gone and only pixels remain.\n\nFour other things decide whether a figure survives production. Type size is specified at final print size: axis labels comfortable in a Word file can fall to about 5 point at an 85 mm column width. Fonts must usually be embedded or converted to outlines. Colour accessibility is now required by many journals. And a panel reproduced from another publication is a permissions matter — on our sample, clearance took eleven days."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I have to choose the journal before you start?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, or we will help you choose one — but formatting cannot begin without a name, because formatting to “a journal” means formatting to an average. The sample pack on this page shows twenty-three deviations between one journal’s instructions and the style it says it uses.\n\nIf you have not chosen, we suggest three candidates with the reason for each — scope, the article types it publishes, typical length, and whether it has published anything resembling your work. Where it has not, we say so.\n\nTwo checks that cost nothing and save weeks: whether the journal invites or commissions your article type; and, for a letter to the editor, whether you are inside its submission window."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Editing and translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Editing and Translation","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/editing-and-translation/manuscript-editing/"}]}];

